--TEST--
An increment is a write, so its target passes the same syntactic gate an assignment's does and a call result is refused where it is written — `rule:types/grammar`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `nvs_ir::lower` desugars `$x++` into the `$x = $x + 1` a compound
// assignment already becomes, so a target it cannot write to is a target it
// cannot read-modify-write either. `nvs_syntax`'s `is_assignable` is the one
// gate every write spelling passes, and PHP refuses the identical shapes at
// parse time — *"Can't use method return value in write context"*. What this
// pins is the agreement: `=`, `+=`, `$x++` and `--$x` over one non-place
// target all answer `E0105`, on the line each is written, rather than three
// of them answering and the fourth reaching a rewrite's own assertion.
class Counter {
    public int $n = 0;

    public function n(): int {
        return $this->n;
    }
}

Counter $c = new Counter();
$c->n() = 1;
$c->n() += 1;
$c->n()++;
--$c->n();
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0105]: this expression cannot be assigned to
   --> case.nvs:19:1
%A
error[E0105]: this expression cannot be assigned to
   --> case.nvs:20:1
%A
error[E0105]: this expression cannot be assigned to
   --> case.nvs:21:1
%A
error[E0105]: this expression cannot be assigned to
   --> case.nvs:22:3
%A
error: aborting due to 4 errors
